cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "acmpilot: adaptive coding and modulation on one optical link under round-trip feedback delay"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-06
license: AGPL-3.0-or-later
abstract: >-
  Rate adaptation on a single free-space optical link in the regime where the
  policy necessarily acts on a channel state that is already stale. A temporally
  correlated fading channel with lognormal and gamma-gamma marginals, driven by a
  stationary first-order Gauss-Markov process so that fade duration and
  level-crossing rate are emergent sample statistics rather than parameters; an
  eight-mode modulation-and-coding ladder spanning 0.498 to 3.749 bit per symbol
  whose switching thresholds are measured by Monte Carlo through the package's own
  modulator and Reed-Solomon bounded-distance accounting, each with a Monte Carlo
  uncertainty of at most 0.045 dB; an explicit round-trip feedback delay against
  which every result is reported; fixed-margin, hysteresis and clairvoyant
  upper-bound selection policies, the last labelled throughout as unachievable by
  any causal policy; mis-selection accounting split into too-aggressive and
  too-conservative with their separate costs; and a learned quantile channel
  predictor whose calibrated confidence output gates aggressive rate choices,
  benchmarked against all three baselines and against an analytic AR(1)
  minimum-mean-square-error predictor on identical seeded sample paths with every
  baseline tuned on held-out data first. The published result is that the learned
  predictor does not beat the analytic one at any feedback delay on either channel
  marginal, and that the confidence gate is worth two orders of magnitude more
  than the choice of predictor. Validation level 3, research grade. Every number
  is a property of this simulator; nothing is validated against measured
  turbulence data. Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for
  operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - adaptive-coding-and-modulation
  - free-space-optics
  - feedback-delay
  - rate-adaptation
  - modcod
  - channel-prediction
  - reed-solomon
  - gamma-gamma-fading
references:
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Andrews
        given-names: "L. C."
      - family-names: Phillips
        given-names: "R. L."
    title: "Laser Beam Propagation through Random Media"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "SPIE Press"
    year: 2005
    notes: >-
      Cited for the weak-turbulence lognormal irradiance model and its validity
      range. No page, equation number or table is quoted from it; the lognormal
      relations used here are the standard ones and are verified numerically in
      validation/validate_channel.py.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Al-Habash
        given-names: "M. A."
      - family-names: Andrews
        given-names: "L. C."
      - family-names: Phillips
        given-names: "R. L."
    title: >-
      Mathematical model for the irradiance probability density function of a
      laser beam propagating through turbulent media
    journal: "Optical Engineering"
    volume: 40
    issue: 8
    year: 2001
    notes: >-
      Cited for the gamma-gamma irradiance model and its scintillation-index
      relation, which this package solves for its shape parameters and then
      verifies numerically rather than asserting.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Rice
        given-names: "S. O."
    title: "Mathematical analysis of random noise"
    journal: "Bell System Technical Journal"
    volume: 23
    year: 1944
    notes: >-
      Cited, together with its 1945 continuation in volume 24, for the
      level-crossing-rate formula used as an analytic cross-check on the emergent
      fade statistics. The package applies it with the derivative variance implied
      by discrete sampling, which is stated in the code and in VALIDATION.md.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Goldsmith
        given-names: "A. J."
      - family-names: Chua
        given-names: "S.-G."
    title: "Variable-rate variable-power MQAM for fading channels"
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Communications"
    volume: 45
    issue: 10
    year: 1997
    notes: >-
      Cited for the adaptive-modulation framing: selecting rate against an
      estimated channel state. This package implements neither its power
      adaptation nor its continuous-rate result; only the framing is borrowed.
  - type: standard
    title: >-
      Digital Video Broadcasting (DVB); Second generation framing structure,
      channel coding and modulation systems for Broadcasting, Interactive
      Services, News Gathering and other broadband satellite applications (DVB-S2)
    number: "ETSI EN 302 307"
    institution:
      name: "ETSI"
    notes: >-
      Named as the canonical real-world adaptive-coding-and-modulation MODCOD
      table, so that a reader wanting standardised thresholds knows where to go.
      This package does not implement DVB-S2, quotes no number from it, and its
      Reed-Solomon thresholds are several dB worse than DVB-S2 LDPC thresholds at
      comparable spectral efficiency.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Proakis
        given-names: "J. G."
      - family-names: Salehi
        given-names: "M."
    title: "Digital Communications"
    publisher:
      name: "McGraw-Hill"
    notes: >-
      Cited for the standard AWGN error-rate expressions for Gray-coded PSK and
      square QAM, used in this package only as checks on its own Monte Carlo
      measurement. No page, edition-specific equation number or table is quoted.
